From dcc88a8da465ddecc9d25ff9774edba37f96dfe3 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: =?UTF-8?q?Martin=20=C5=BDdila?= <m.zdila@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 4 Sep 2026 09:21:29 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] trail colours: hiking_colours and bike_colours from route
 relations

Two 9-bit masks, one bit per TrailColour, filled from the waymark colour of the
route relations an edge belongs to: hiking_colours from route=hiking|foot,
bike_colours from route=bicycle|mtb. Exposed like any other encoded value, so
`details=hiking_colours` returns them and a custom model can read them.

A mask rather than an enum because a way routinely carries several routes of
different colours, and a single dominant colour is a rendering decision the
client should make, not one the graph should bake in. OSMReader hands each
relation the flags accumulated from the way's earlier relations
(OSMReader.preprocessRelations), so the bits simply OR together and relation
order never matters -- unlike the network parsers, this one has no conflict to
resolve.

Colour comes from the first field of osmc:symbol, falling back to colour (then
color). The fallback is not the rare path: across Europe 69 % of route=hiking
relations carry osmc:symbol against 18 % with colour, while for route=bicycle it
is 2 % against 16 %. Reading either tag alone would miss most of one mode. An
unparsable symbol field falls through to the colour tag rather than stopping,
so a value like 'greenbl' cannot hide a good colour=green beside it.

Nine bits: the eight colours that actually occur on European route relations
(red, blue, green, yellow, black, orange, gray, white) plus OTHER for the
recognisable rest -- brown, purple, teal and the hex values. Those are each too
rare to justify a bit, but collapsing them keeps such a way marked instead of
dropping it. grey and gray share a bit; both spellings are live.

MTB folds into bike_colours rather than taking a third mask: 16k route=mtb
relations in Europe against 245k route=hiking, only 4k of them coloured, and a
separate mask would only buy the case where an mtb and a cycle route of
different colours share a way.

